#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723521 :question/problem "Given $f(t)=- 3 t^{3}\\,i+(3 t^{2} - 2)\\,j+2 t\\,k$, find $(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Use the dot product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime\\prime}(t)\\cdot f(t)+f^{\\prime}(t)\\cdot f^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t^{3})=- 9 t^{2} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{2} - 2)=6 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)=2 \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =- 9 t^{2}\\,i+6 t\\,j+2\\,k \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 9 t^{2})=- 18 t \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(6 t)=6 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2)=0 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}(t) & =- 18 t\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(- 18 t)(- 3 t^{3})+(6)(3 t^{2} - 2)+(0)(2 t) \\\\  & =54 t^{4} + 18 t^{2} - 12 \\\\ P_2 & =(- 9 t^{2})(- 9 t^{2})+(6 t)(6 t)+(2)(2) \\\\  & =81 t^{4} + 36 t^{2} + 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f^{\\prime}\\cdot f)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =135 t^{4} + 54 t^{2} - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723548 :question/problem "Given $f(t)=⟨3 t^{2} - 1,\\,- 3 t - 3,\\,3 t^{3} - 3⟩$, find $(f^{\\prime}\\times f)^{\\prime}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Use the cross product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f^{\\prime}\\times f)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime\\prime}(t)\\times f(t)+f^{\\prime}(t)\\times f^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{2} - 1)=6 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t - 3)=-3 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{3} - 3)=9 t^{2} \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨6 t,\\,-3,\\,9 t^{2}⟩ \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(6 t)=6 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(-3)=0 \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(9 t^{2})=18 t \\\\ f^{\\prime\\prime}(t) & =⟨6,\\,0,\\,18 t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 & 0 & 18 t \\\\ 3 t^{2} - 1 & - 3 t - 3 & 3 t^{3} - 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{1,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 18 t \\\\ - 3 t - 3 & 3 t^{3} - 3\\end{vmatrix}=54 t^{2} + 54 t \\\\ P_{1,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 18 t \\\\ 3 t^{2} - 1 & 3 t^{3} - 3\\end{vmatrix}=36 t^{3} - 18 t + 18 \\\\ P_{1,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 3 t^{2} - 1 & - 3 t - 3\\end{vmatrix}=- 18 t - 18 \\\\ P_1 & =⟨54 t^{2} + 54 t,\\,36 t^{3} - 18 t + 18,\\,- 18 t - 18⟩ \\\\ P_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 6 t & -3 & 9 t^{2} \\\\ 6 t & -3 & 9 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{2,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 9 t^{2} \\\\ -3 & 9 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=0 \\\\ P_{2,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}6 t & 9 t^{2} \\\\ 6 t & 9 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=0 \\\\ P_{2,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 t & -3 \\\\ 6 t & -3\\end{vmatrix}=0 \\\\ P_2 & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f^{\\prime}\\times f)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =⟨54 t^{2} + 54 t,\\,36 t^{3} - 18 t + 18,\\,- 18 t - 18⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723551 :question/problem "Find $(f\\times g)^{\\prime}(t)$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 t^{2} \\\\ t + 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ g(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t - 1 \\\\ - 3 t^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the cross product rule, preserving the factor order:\n\n$$\n(f\\times g)^{\\prime}(t)=f^{\\prime}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times g^{\\prime}(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(-3)=0 \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})=4 t \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 2)=1 \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)=1 \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 1)=1 \\\\ g^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- 3 t^{2})=- 6 t \\\\ g^{\\prime}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ - 6 t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 t & 1 \\\\ t & t - 1 & - 3 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{1,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 t & 1 \\\\ t - 1 & - 3 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=- 12 t^{3} - t + 1 \\\\ P_{1,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ t & - 3 t^{2}\\end{vmatrix}=t \\\\ P_{1,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 t \\\\ t & t - 1\\end{vmatrix}=- 4 t^{2} \\\\ P_1 & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 12 t^{3} - t + 1 \\\\ t \\\\ - 4 t^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ P_2 & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 2 t^{2} & t + 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & - 6 t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ P_{2,1} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 t^{2} & t + 2 \\\\ 1 & - 6 t\\end{vmatrix}=- 12 t^{3} - t - 2 \\\\ P_{2,2} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & t + 2 \\\\ 1 & - 6 t\\end{vmatrix}=2 - 17 t \\\\ P_{2,3} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 t^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=- 2 t^{2} - 3 \\\\ P_2 & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 12 t^{3} - t - 2 \\\\ 2 - 17 t \\\\ - 2 t^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\times g)^{\\prime}(t) & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}- 24 t^{3} - 2 t - 1 \\\\ 2 - 16 t \\\\ - 6 t^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723555 :question/problem "Find $(v(t)f(t))^{\\prime}$ given that $f(t)=⟨e^{- t},\\,e^{3 t},\\,e^{t + 3}⟩$ and $v(t)=e^{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar function multiple rule:\n\n$$\n(v(t)f(t))^{\\prime}=v(t)f^{\\prime}(t)+v^{\\prime}(t)f(t)\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}_{1}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{- t})=- e^{- t} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{2}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{3 t})=3 e^{3 t} \\\\ f^{\\prime}_{3}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t + 3})=e^{t + 3} \\\\ f^{\\prime}(t) & =⟨- e^{- t},\\,3 e^{3 t},\\,e^{t + 3}⟩ \\\\ v^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(e^{t})=e^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the first product-rule term and the second product-rule term, denoted $P_1$ and $P_2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_1 & =(e^{t})⟨- e^{- t},\\,3 e^{3 t},\\,e^{t + 3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,3 e^{4 t},\\,e^{t} e^{t + 3}⟩ \\\\ P_2 & =(e^{t})⟨e^{- t},\\,e^{3 t},\\,e^{t + 3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,e^{4 t},\\,e^{t} e^{t + 3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the two terms componentwise:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v(t)f(t))^{\\prime} & =P_1+P_2 \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4 e^{4 t},\\,2 e^{2 t + 3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723562 :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa427c6b7ece0f686452452cdac619cda3d8a55fc7574d2772bf7462468d6d99.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $1-0=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723563 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc75ddae3094fd68394735ab7684151688cc347239196bd6391fb7a306163bf4.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}, 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n0\\le t\\le 2 \\pi.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $1-0=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}, 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723576 :question/problem "![](images/86/86ce51e0ca19867f08bb53d0d61acc330e892e34e5335eb14cea6241b8a618b8.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $0-(-1)=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C -y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723578 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efde07cb0764baca4a62fcb9355a71e31987c4e8b7842e1b4b0d739eb8a45ef7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $0-(-1)=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C -y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=-y(t)x^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723585 :question/problem "![](images/70/705db77de56d47d65f80770a9d1a3a3f27b53f992c88b2351f8a320e251bd52f.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by $C$, with\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\le t\\le \\frac{5 \\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nUse $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$. Determine the **pointwise integrand** obtained from this field and parametrization, with the sign adjusted for orientation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{3}}\\boxed{\\ ?\\ }\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The scalar curl is $1-0=1$. The boundary is positively oriented, so we use its circulation without an extra minus sign.\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)=\\langle - 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 8 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}, 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the surviving field component, including the orientation sign, and multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=x(t)y^{\\prime}(t)\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the missing pointwise expression is $\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723599 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem in flux form to calculate the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle\\sin y+2x,\\ e^x-y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le2,\\ 0\\le y\\le1\\}$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1.\n$$\n\nUse\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangle $D$ and its boundary are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals 0 to 2 and y equals 0 to 1](images/ea/ea6c25e40b6683d9ff4a4884dbbda708fd79dbd79d0b50730f9ad6e165d2eb58.png)\n\nThe term $\\sin y$ is constant when differentiating $P$ with respect to $x$, and $e^x$ is constant when differentiating $Q$ with respect to $y$. Thus the given derivatives yield divergence $2+(-1)=1$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_0^2(2-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[x]_0^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[2y]_0^1=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, constant divergence $1$ times the rectangle's area $2\\cdot1$ gives outward flux $2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723609 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle e^y+x^2y,\\ \\cos x+xy^2+2y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(-1,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$, and $(-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices give $D=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le2,\\ 0\\le y\\le1\\}$, shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals minus 1 to 2 and y equals 0 to 1](images/f6/f6437eddec7d44a10c7fb2b3461a09844ca06e5131b69503f51432eb87fedd31.png)\n\nHere $P=e^y+x^2y$ and $Q=\\cos x+xy^2+2y$. Holding the other variable constant gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2xy,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2xy+2,\n\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4xy+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_0^1\\int_{-1}^2(4xy+2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[2x^2y+2x]_{-1}^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl((8y+4)-(2y-2)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(6y+6)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[3y^2+6y]_0^1=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth parts contribute: the $4xy$ term integrates to $3$, and constant divergence $2$ contributes $2$ times the rectangle's area $3$, giving $6$. Their sum is $9$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723614 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle\\cos y-x^2,\\ \\sin x+3y\\right\\rangle\n$$\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le2,\\ -1\\le y\\le1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle and let $C=\\partial D$ be traversed counterclockwise, with outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. The components $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D$. Green's theorem in flux form gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangle $D$ and its boundary are shown below.\n\n![Rectangle from x equals 0 to 2 and y equals minus 1 to 1](images/2f/2f3374de4a29c62587a79f6286dfe788e64c37e09e9d9b83b3a67b4dac10dc21.png)\n\nHere $P=\\cos y-x^2$ and $Q=\\sin x+3y$. The cross-variable terms contribute zero to the relevant partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=-2x,\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=3.\n$$\n\nUsing $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le2,\\ -1\\le y\\le1\\}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^1\\int_0^2(3-2x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^1[3x-x^2]_0^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^1(6-4)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=[2y]_{-1}^1=2-(-2)=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inner integral is $2$; the outer integral accounts for the full height $1-(-1)=2$.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723694 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)},- \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/95/952295caff46f0cb7de608127484a2d5bc2474516df18793c772d67ab7f8c3b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=12 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}3 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(4 t)=2\\sin(2 t)\\cos(2 t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(2 t)$, so $du=-2\\sin(2 t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-12\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=6\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=2\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=2\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-4\\right)=4.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723700 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/90/900a1558522e97f234029e84361c00f514091b3d67cbb7373fa3c3faa53b88ed.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is counterclockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=8 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(t)$, so $du=-1\\sin(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=24\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=-24\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=-8\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=-8\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(16\\right)=16.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723710 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/7d/7d9ab5c8f992e07fdfbba34a0e3ed4d3df56fc3f9002912229e75b7f33bf8bb6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=-y$, $Q=0$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-\\oint_C y\\,dx\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}-\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\sin(t)$, so $du=1\\cos(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=12\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=12\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=4\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=4\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-8\\right)=8.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723711 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)},- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq t\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/6f/6f382bf2e83e21a77c17f98a20d5d62b0efe8f5e1308f037f47032475abf5490.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is counterclockwise. Choose $P=0$, $Q=x$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\oint_C x\\,dy\\right).\n$$\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\sin(t)$, so $du=1\\cos(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-16\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=-16\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=- \\frac{16}{3}\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=- \\frac{16}{3}\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=1\\left(\\frac{32}{3}\\right)=\\frac{32}{3}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723718 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$.\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\leq t\\leq \\pi.\n$$\n\nThe arrows indicate increasing parameter.\n\n![The shaded region and its oriented boundary.](images/90/906f41119591337834b399bdad98dd3484878d5913f0aaddfbc2bf62d04b90f2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The boundary is clockwise. Choose $P=-y$, $Q=0$, so $\\partial Q/\\partial x-\\partial P/\\partial y=1$. Thus\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-\\oint_C y\\,dx\\right).\n$$\nThe minus sign corrects the negative orientation.\nOn $C$, $x'(t)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y'(t)=- 6 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}$. \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}-\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sin(2 t)=2\\sin(t)\\cos(t).\n$$\nUse $u=\\cos(t)$, so $du=-1\\sin(t)\\,dt$. The limits for $u$ are $1$ and $-1$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J_1&=-12\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\,dt\\\\&=12\\int_1^{-1}u^2\\,du\\\\&=4\\left[u^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\&=4\\left((-1)^3-1^3\\right)=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(D)=-1\\left(-8\\right)=8.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723725 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;- y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=- y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/3e/3e25c6423bcef348cfd0ac91b32d4d65c3ae980def82d8e220d5bff659193936.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(1\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(-2\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723730 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x^{2} y - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\;e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$, and $x=2$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- 2 x^{2} y - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=e^{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=- 4 x y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=- 4 x y.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=2$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/a1/a1c6dfa6dd998451b9caf7772e1b1931a780cc689c8fa301916c7498adf005f2.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\sqrt{x}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{x}}\\left(- 4 x y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[- 2 x y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\sqrt{x}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(- 8 x^{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(- 8 x^{2}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- \\frac{8 x^{3}}{3}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\&=\\left(- \\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=- \\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $- \\frac{64}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723743 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - x + y,\\;x^{2} - y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} - y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=-1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=-1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=-2.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/82/8253ae5644e9229ad0fd91023cd296b7dfca96f1d089d4e776c496898983737d.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(-2\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[- 2 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(2\\right) \\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723748 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region between $y=0$ and $y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=3 x + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=3,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=5.\n$$\n\nOn the stated interval, $2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\geq0$. Use vertical slices from $y=0$ to $y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/78/786e6657e805e8c3968dbd64180e4d0405254c7649fc8213598a803f05ad0d19.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq x\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}\\left(5\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[5 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left[\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[10 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right]_{x=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\&=\\left(10\\right)-\\left(-10\\right) \\\\&=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $20$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723754 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x^{2} + y,\\;x^{2} - 3 x y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=x^{2}$, and $x=2$.\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=3 x^{2} + y$ and $Q=x^{2} - 3 x y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=6 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=- 3 x,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3 x.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=2$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=x^{2}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/5b/5b29e3b24bc18633df2ab03028bfd2449e7eaf4b9f2dda0a3ab78b0ea366008c.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[3 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(3 x^{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(3 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[\\frac{3 x^{4}}{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\&=\\left(12\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $12$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723761 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x + y,\\;x^{2} + 2 y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(-4,2)$, and $(4,2)$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 2,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/f1/f1808908b559896a7a9cdc76067304d66a52e6eb58a250fe4164e14b27085d27.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} + 2 y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=2,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3.\n$$\n\nThe lower vertex has $y=0$ and the top edge has $y=2$. Horizontal slices run between the two sloping sides, from $x=- 2 y$ to $x=2 y$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/f1/f1808908b559896a7a9cdc76067304d66a52e6eb58a250fe4164e14b27085d27.png)\n\nThis is a type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 2,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{- 2 y}^{2 y}\\left(3\\right)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[3 x\\right]_{x=- 2 y}^{x=2 y}\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\right]\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(12 y\\right)\\,dy \\\\&=\\left[6 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\&=\\left(24\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $24$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723768 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\;y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=x^{5}$, and $x=1$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{5}\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/89/89edc4d1c65965d1d423115458d583001e73f18554950d2d85d04b75ac002c96.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=x - \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=y + e^{x}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=1$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=x^{5}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/89/89edc4d1c65965d1d423115458d583001e73f18554950d2d85d04b75ac002c96.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{5}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{5}}\\left(2\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[2 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{5}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(2 x^{5}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 x^{5}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[\\frac{x^{6}}{3}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $\\frac{1}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723774 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x + y,\\;x^{2} + y\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(-2,1)$, and $(2,1)$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 1,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/dd/dde0c524fe9351f830a5daf49e507142650cdd854bdd52744a3b21208634933f.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=2 x + y$ and $Q=x^{2} + y$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=1,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3.\n$$\n\nThe lower vertex has $y=0$ and the top edge has $y=1$. Horizontal slices run between the two sloping sides, from $x=- 2 y$ to $x=2 y$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/dd/dde0c524fe9351f830a5daf49e507142650cdd854bdd52744a3b21208634933f.png)\n\nThis is a type II region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq y\\leq 1,\\quad - 2 y\\leq x\\leq 2 y\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{- 2 y}^{2 y}\\left(3\\right)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[3 x\\right]_{x=- 2 y}^{x=2 y}\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- 6 y\\right)\\right]\\,dy \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 y\\right)\\,dy \\\\&=\\left[6 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\&=\\left(6\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723786 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the **outward flux** of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\;- x y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=0$, $y=2 x^{3}$, and $x=1$.\n\nUse the supplied bounds and formula:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 x^{3}\\}\n$$\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/7f/7ff77b6a682420607a6188c73de2940a434c4ce920b4e15e7e0e7d531e3bfc16.png)\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nSelect the flux." :question/worked-solution "The components have continuous first derivatives on a neighborhood of $D$. With $C=\\partial D$ counterclockwise and $\\mathbf n$ outward, Green\\'s theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nHere $P=- 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=- x y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=- 4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=- x,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=- 5 x.\n$$\n\nThe curved boundary meets $y=0$ at the origin. Up to $x=1$, vertical slices run from $y=0$ to $y=2 x^{3}$.\n\n![Shaded region and a representative integration slice.](images/7f/7ff77b6a682420607a6188c73de2940a434c4ce920b4e15e7e0e7d531e3bfc16.png)\n\nThis is a type I region:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 2 x^{3}\\}\n$$\n\nIntegrating and substituting both inner limits gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{2 x^{3}}\\left(- 5 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[- 5 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2 x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[\\left(- 10 x^{4}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right]\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 10 x^{4}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[- 2 x^{5}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(-2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outward flux is $-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723789 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(0,-2)$, $(2,-2)$, $(2,0)$, $(0,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle 3 x y^{3},\\ 2 x y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=8 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(3 y^{3})+(8 x y^{3})\\\\ &=8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 2,\\ -2\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/41/4174dc632b4b98c4c2650e42101b10ffbd28836e09d9ffd042c53812855376ba.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{0}^{2}(8 x y^{3} + 3 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723795 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-3,-3)$, $(-1,-3)$, $(-1,0)$, $(-3,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x^{2} y^{2},\\ 2 x y\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 x y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 x y^{2})+(2 x)\\\\ &=- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\leq x\\leq -1,\\ -3\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/43/432f70bfcb8fbd0d0edec65352a8c286be3f3a2ca4becce569f2e8d912d03aa6.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{-1}\\int_{-3}^{0}(- 2 x y^{2} + 2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723796 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-1,-1)$, $(0,-1)$, $(0,2)$, $(-1,2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x y^{3},\\ 3 x y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=12 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- y^{3})+(12 x y^{3})\\\\ &=12 x y^{3} - y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ -1\\leq y\\leq 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/26/26d43b65f45dfa30023a33369d5c2d184641a3e5f83e63e251078685f5328dc6.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_{-1}^{2}(12 x y^{3} - y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723802 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-3,1)$, $(0,1)$, $(0,2)$, $(-3,2)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - 2 x y^{3},\\ 2 x y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=8 x y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 y^{3})+(8 x y^{3})\\\\ &=8 x y^{3} - 2 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-3\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ 1\\leq y\\leq 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/ad/ad86eea1220a388c4f2240f4279dd2e6f7e31ad3d31a0dcc22090e3572ab3c3a.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{1}^{2}(8 x y^{3} - 2 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723803 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the rectangle with vertices $(-2,-3)$, $(0,-3)$, $(0,0)$, $(-2,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - 2 x^{2} y - 3 x y^{3},\\ x y^{2} - 2 y^{4}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 4 x y - 3 y^{3}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 x y - 8 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 4 x y - 3 y^{3})+(2 x y - 8 y^{3})\\\\ &=- 2 x y - 11 y^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular coordinate ranges give\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\leq x\\leq 0,\\ -3\\leq y\\leq 0\\}.\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/b8/b89a4729afdaf568863a36b2d98b478c9ceb637a7eee2cdd8ee9168477b5c592.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-3}^{0}\\int_{-2}^{0}(- 2 x y - 11 y^{3})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723830 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14$ and $x=2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - 3 x^{2} - 3 y^{3},\\ - x y - 2 y^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 6 x\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- x - 4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 6 x)+(- x - 4 y)\\\\ &=- 7 x - 4 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2&=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14\\\\ 4\\left(y - 1\\right)^2&=16\\\\ y&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2$ and the upper boundary is $x=- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1&\\leq y\\leq 3,\\\\ 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2&\\leq x\\leq - 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/0e/0ec4207925a9b0019e058d5a35b3cf14f5face57c304291ecb624808984469f5.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_{2 y^{2} - 4 y + 2}^{- 2 y^{2} + 4 y + 14}(- 7 x - 4 y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723838 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=y^{2} + 2 y + 1$ and $x=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle x^{2} y - x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} - y^{3}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=2 x y - y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y - 3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(2 x y - y^{2})+(- 4 x y - 3 y^{2})\\\\ &=- 2 x y - 4 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2} + 2 y + 1&=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1\\\\ 3\\left(y + 1\\right)^2&=3\\\\ y&=-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=y^{2} + 2 y + 1$ and the upper boundary is $x=- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2&\\leq y\\leq 0,\\\\ y^{2} + 2 y + 1&\\leq x\\leq - 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/37/3752f342d80decb31e6020ab992998b9090b90396a8b0dac97ca5d3afb132ddc.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{0}\\int_{y^{2} + 2 y + 1}^{- 2 y^{2} - 4 y + 1}(- 2 x y - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723840 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $y=x^{2}$ and $y=8 - x^{2}$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle - x^{2} y + 2 x y^{2},\\ - 2 x y^{2} + y^{3}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=- 2 x y + 2 y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y + 3 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(- 2 x y + 2 y^{2})+(- 4 x y + 3 y^{2})\\\\ &=- 6 x y + 5 y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}&=8 - x^{2}\\\\ 2\\left(x\\right)^2&=8\\\\ x&=-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $y=x^{2}$ and the upper boundary is $y=8 - x^{2}$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2&\\leq x\\leq 2,\\\\ x^{2}&\\leq y\\leq 8 - x^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/87/87f9487571eb2b33b122f986c434756500d25d2d11be5d81d13d808ab15cfeb9.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{2}\\int_{x^{2}}^{8 - x^{2}}(- 6 x y + 5 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186723846 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the counterclockwise boundary of the finite region enclosed by the parabolas $x=y^{2}$ and $x=2 - y^{2}$. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the outward flux of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle 2 x^{2} y - 2 x y,\\ - 2 x y^{2} + y^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral in the order $\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Choose the integral whose integrand is given directly by this theorem and whose bounds describe the enclosed region. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, Green's theorem in flux form states\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x)\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial components have continuous first partial derivatives on a neighborhood of the enclosed region. The counterclockwise boundary has positive orientation.\n\nThe matching component derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x y - 2 y\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=- 4 x y + 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd these derivatives to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=(4 x y - 2 y)+(- 4 x y + 2 y)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the two boundaries and completing the square gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y^{2}&=2 - y^{2}\\\\ 2\\left(y\\right)^2&=2\\\\ y&=-1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad y=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBetween these intersections, the lower inner-coordinate boundary is $x=y^{2}$ and the upper boundary is $x=2 - y^{2}$. The region $D$ is described by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1&\\leq y\\leq 1,\\\\ y^{2}&\\leq x\\leq 2 - y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![Enclosed region with coordinate axes and its boundary.](images/c9/c962eee590078fb59fda8760e0892554dad8cb02da5a360054730717797f9065.png)\n\nPutting the integrand and bounds together in the requested order gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{y^{2}}^{2 - y^{2}}(0)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724051 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle - x + e^{y},\\ y^{2} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-2\\le x\\le 1$, $0\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-2\\le x\\le 1,\\ 0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -2, 1 and y bounds 0, 1](images/09/090c7ca9a0a029a46dd3a2d324cabd3632574173e85baf6b464f987e3a86e697.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=-1,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2 y - 1.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-2}^{1}\\left(2 y - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[2 x y - x\\right]_{x=-2}^{x=1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(6 y - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[3 y^{2} - 3 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724068 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(y \\right)},\\ - 2 y + e^{x}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $1\\le x\\le 2$, $-1\\le y\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):1\\le x\\le 2,\\ -1\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds 1, 2 and y bounds -1, 1](images/5a/5a7482c055aec608ccc33a59139a65384c3a3f2aaebb15ebbf03c7ac785e59b2.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 x - 2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(4 x - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left[2 x^{2} - 2 x\\right]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[4 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=1}\\\\\n&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(-4\\right)\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724074 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle x + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ 2 y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 2$, $0\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\ 0\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 2 and y bounds 0, 2](images/30/306187a2ff03bdf4b7f3c52090b8b5b353902213d297292f200e839df7fea8e4.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=1,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=4 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 y + 1.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(4 y + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left[4 x y + x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(12 y + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[6 y^{2} + 3 y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(30\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724083 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + e^{y},\\ - 2 y + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le 0$, $-1\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\ -1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds -1, 0 and y bounds -1, 2](images/4c/4cb4ba6d860090403990851d7c2744e25f477a3f3569e9ef8194b7e3dc389137.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=4 x,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=4 x - 2.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(4 x - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left[2 x^{2} - 2 x\\right]_{x=-1}^{x=0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(-4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 y\\right]_{y=-1}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(-8\\right)-\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=-12.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186724084 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the outward flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left\\langle 3 x y^{3} + \\cos{\\left(y \\right)},\\ y^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the rectangle $0\\le x\\le 2$, $1\\le y\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the rectangle, with $C=\\partial D$ traversed counterclockwise and outward unit normal $\\mathbf n$. Both components have continuous first derivatives near $D$. Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\\\\n&=\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\\n&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rectangular bounds are\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\ 1\\le y\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n![Rectangle with x bounds 0, 2 and y bounds 1, 2](images/9c/9c9655873e8faf6bbad28c0711dc8735d37040e10ac780e5b422414331eed7a6.png)\n\nHold the other coordinate constant in each partial derivative. The cross-variable terms contribute zero.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}&=3 y^{3},\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}&=2 y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the matching partials gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=3 y^{3} + 2 y.\n$$\n\nIntegrate with respect to $x$, subtract its endpoints, then integrate with respect to $y$ and subtract its endpoints.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Phi&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(3 y^{3} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left[3 x y^{3} + 2 x y\\right]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(6 y^{3} + 4 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 y^{4}}{2} + 2 y^{2}\\right]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\\\\n&=\\left(32\\right)-\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{57}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
